#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29d1479d-8b8d-5bbb-bb1d-3e4a244cb137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277278} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf4139c-b2ca-5302-89d4-ab02d05b733f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277279} {:answer/id #uuid "3e82a6db-a670-5b09-bb4d-afd3cb9f40be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277280} {:answer/id #uuid "bea7083f-5aaa-5edd-9dee-9e0640acf362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277281} {:answer/id #uuid "90efd263-4e21-5a4c-be10-bd1febf0ea78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcf4139c-b2ca-5302-89d4-ab02d05b733f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277279} :answer-field/id #uuid "76db370d-a620-5984-a449-0cfdc8dc982b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eb95820b-48e7-589b-b453-db9d5d27848d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112964" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=20{x}^{5}-7{x}^{4}+x-4,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $20,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 5,$ $\\pm 10$ and $\\pm 20.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{20}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{10}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{20}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{10}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to the lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{20}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{20},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{10},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7614a05-f221-5fda-b491-6893f44a1a5f" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with Multiple Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5852f3fa-1509-517d-918f-e9399357ea6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{g}{3{h}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277285} {:answer/id #uuid "b79330e2-2190-5dde-807b-d4dd36d62d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{g}^{2}}{3h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277286} {:answer/id #uuid "a2aa00d6-1cf0-511d-b817-de62406915cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{h}^{2}}{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277287} {:answer/id #uuid "e3758285-9f52-5c48-83a2-1eba067b94e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{g{h}^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277288} {:answer/id #uuid "dd121968-abe1-58d1-baf3-51275f7e9f2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3g}{{h}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5852f3fa-1509-517d-918f-e9399357ea6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{g}{3{h}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277285} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecc0b8cc-ff19-5dab-aa5c-9928ba09585b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a48aa76-c7fd-50b2-ab17-7f90486ab6d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-113824" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{4{g}^{2}}{7{h}^{3}}=a$ and $\\frac{12g}{7h}=b,$ what is $a÷b?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\na÷b=\\frac{4{g}^{2}}{7{h}^{3}}÷\\frac{12g}{7h},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{g}^{2}}{7{h}^{3}}÷\\frac{12g}{7h} & = \\\\ \\frac{4{g}^{2}}{7{h}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{7h}{12g} & = \\\\ \\frac{4g\\cdot g}{7h\\cdot {h}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{7h}{3\\cdot 4g} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4g}\\cdot g}{\\cancel{7h}\\cdot {h}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{7h}}{3\\cdot \\cancel{4g}} & = \\\\ \\frac{g}{3{h}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94cbd132-72fc-5645-bca3-9c81b6ac3845" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Rule of Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae07c290-23e0-52d1-95c9-b380b57d031a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277292} {:answer/id #uuid "b31136a5-eaf4-5204-ad5d-af3ae64355bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277293} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef6b23f-604f-5240-8d10-c59c2a0cb082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277294} {:answer/id #uuid "2b590086-e3b7-5da3-87b0-d6206eb0052e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277295} {:answer/id #uuid "850e4ac8-2c5a-5363-b704-e476ae06ea6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b590086-e3b7-5da3-87b0-d6206eb0052e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277295} :answer-field/id #uuid "27c4f0b8-3764-5138-ba84-e50d86d49c0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad69f963-f40c-56bd-95d6-2dfbeeffb73d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122084" :question/problem "Linda wants to buy a refreshing drink. The store has $6$ types of sodas, $6$ types of juices, and $3$ types of water. How many choices of drinks does Linda have?" :question/worked-solution "In order to find the total number of choices Linda has, we can use the **rule of sum**:\n\n$$\n6+6+3=15\n$$\n\nTherefore, Linda has $15$ choices of drinks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Product of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbed6fe-e67e-519e-8d16-d23e07b37b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277298} {:answer/id #uuid "970c727b-5391-5e23-8e4f-b5762579af66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277299} {:answer/id #uuid "0efafccd-3dbf-5c2d-a483-35b87103e117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277300} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a7cf08-da54-5f93-915f-037a866067b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277301} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1e3f06-2e8a-5995-a897-4290e1b746d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c1e3f06-2e8a-5995-a897-4290e1b746d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277302} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3d1e849-3022-5eb8-93a3-6a5fe5ac7a52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "939510b6-5bc7-5d08-b915-1376d3819fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-288814" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-5x-14=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=7,\\,b=-5,\\,c=-14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-14)}{7}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fcd4b38-1446-5ae3-97ad-552f58e65adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fcd4b38-1446-5ae3-97ad-552f58e65adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277305} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e728ba8-2a52-51e7-8d74-28dc61c5396a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a7fff84-7ff5-5fce-a049-e02df71c9444" :question/math-academy-id "q-338378" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964743/assets/601562e6fab230b818a1776e42b700518f803f425dcb7f407d3659e91db0ff33.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)+2)^{2}}{(f(x)-2)^{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)+2)^{2}}{(f(x)-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)+2))}^{2}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-2))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=1.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)+2)=3(1)+2=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-2)=1-2=-1\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)+2)^{2}}{(f(x)-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{25}{-1}=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbe35c7b-8068-5b28-a820-d25e6b62565a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots Using the Quadratic Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "457e804b-10aa-59ee-88ad-3a839f73934f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277308} {:answer/id #uuid "feaefaee-d395-5bc6-920a-c159d21b355e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277309} {:answer/id #uuid "f33946c4-048f-5adf-8e5e-b5aa8bc2392c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277310} {:answer/id #uuid "785de841-e30d-5554-ae18-9a5bcd989d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277311} {:answer/id #uuid "f7dbe447-f2c7-5e0d-bdb3-f03c81cfa689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1\\pm \\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "457e804b-10aa-59ee-88ad-3a839f73934f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277308} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c23881-8e59-5522-abe3-28fec3edc201" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79f12e46-deae-53cd-89d3-2dfcd47a8848" :question/math-academy-id "q-66428" :question/problem "Solve  the equation $\\,-{x}^{2}-2x-17=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=-1,b=-2,$ and $c=-17.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(-1)(-17)}}{2(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4-68}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}}{-2} \\\\  & =-1\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-1+4\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-1-4\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5337e4-9289-52fc-ba01-a0e7a8df6285" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "969492db-04f1-50ee-98b8-df93cd718f2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(p^{2} + 1)(p - 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077818} {:answer/id #uuid "e012d9f8-0e75-5fe3-a86c-27e997111d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(p^{2} + 1)(p + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077819} {:answer/id #uuid "e0308bff-5694-50cc-ade9-d83799b03aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(p + 1)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077820} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b636e0-a82e-52e9-90a6-876ed60ec41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(p^{2} + 1)(p + 1)(p - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077821} {:answer/id #uuid "55bf765c-f379-5b8e-8f36-1f53380f8b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(p - 1)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8b636e0-a82e-52e9-90a6-876ed60ec41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(p^{2} + 1)(p + 1)(p - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077821} :answer-field/id #uuid "88749bf8-a216-56c1-9d3d-96966d0bad80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2efcd2a0-1d52-5170-a931-7391dea9b4b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-69271" :question/problem "Factor the expression ${p}^{4}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{p}^{4}-1={({p}^{2})}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({p}^{2})}^{2}-{1}^{2}=({p}^{2}+1)({p}^{2}-1)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({p}^{2}+1)({p}^{2}-1) & =({p}^{2}+1)({p}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =({p}^{2}+1)(p+1)(p-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "add45606-39f6-5a61-9535-867d2950d183" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Factor Rule to Integrate a Power Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cea061c8-19da-56ce-8d54-dcb7e0787784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277315} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8fa5c2-a40d-5a57-be22-aaf96873e790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{6}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277316} {:answer/id #uuid "55b36c66-5fdf-57f6-86a5-50daa89c7b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277317} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fad63a-6769-5a64-bb98-24501b359c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{x}^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277318} {:answer/id #uuid "d7af0af2-f981-5a4e-b8c4-3c6596a9d81a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{5}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cea061c8-19da-56ce-8d54-dcb7e0787784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277315} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d4d1c21-d2ac-550b-84dd-1ef0c1984175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "820f101a-d00c-5982-8568-97f2eedadddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-71164" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{5+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a560db4d-e9d3-5d89-b2cd-c886f32db9df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da385ac4-15bb-5baa-ad0d-ef1ed56a3a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277322} {:answer/id #uuid "0370af9b-2364-5f2d-b604-099c3aaed1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277323} {:answer/id #uuid "693715a4-a3d0-5def-85bd-e613ba22256a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277324} {:answer/id #uuid "a82e2052-e69d-510f-8cb9-c2f1dda72994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277325} {:answer/id #uuid "d562c841-bb3b-5e85-a962-7eef2551a082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "693715a4-a3d0-5def-85bd-e613ba22256a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277324} :answer-field/id #uuid "af3e69f7-bad0-529a-b47f-8633cbb54286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12a7aa4e-d3f3-5b8b-8e46-e7bcc341c503" :question/math-academy-id "q-74715" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-3-2\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964743/assets/0c6548a95b2a81c7dcde33ead78ee0be8d5cee936edc1e2a98c2d59d259404b7.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})≈0.588\\,00.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.588\\,00≈3.73\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3ee7d83-2897-53ea-aa8f-05d52cb1f9e7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae31f3d8-4162-53e1-8733-8424d19cc1c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277329} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a189a5-88ca-5c2b-9207-5a320fe84315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277330} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1b56a4-e52e-5580-a7cb-203a11983782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277331} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2fe3cf-242a-5a9b-8374-54de458e4a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277332} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d1ba45-805a-5b16-aa41-a4bb49678f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae31f3d8-4162-53e1-8733-8424d19cc1c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277329} :answer-field/id #uuid "2149e681-c6ca-582b-9627-bc70ee3e5460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ce617aa-5397-5565-9c42-e78f189d502a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98132" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $A(-5,2,9)$ and $B(-1,-2,9)?$" :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(-5,2,9)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(-1,-2,9)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(-1-(-5))^{2}+(-2-2)^{2}+(9-9)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{32} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
